    {
      "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017-sch-1-sec-26wg",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "26WG Whether access or disclosure would be likely, or would not be",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017#sch-1-sec-26wg"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017.pdf, p.14",
      "values:originalText": "likely, to result in serious harm—relevant matters\n\nFor the purposes of this Division, in determining whether a\nreasonable person would conclude that an access to, or a disclosure\nof, information:\n\n(a) would be likely; or\n\n(b) would not be likely;\nto result in serious harm to any of the individuals to whom the\ninformation relates, have regard to the following:\n\n(c) the kind or kinds of information;\n\n(d) the sensitivity of the information;\n\n(e) whether the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures;\n\n(f) if the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures—the likelihood that any of those security measures\ncould be overcome;\n\n(g) the persons, or the kinds of persons, who have obtained, or\nwho could obtain, the information;\n\n(h) if a security technology or methodology:\n\n(i) was used in relation to the information; and\n\n(ii) was designed to make the information unintelligible or\nmeaningless to persons who are not authorised to obtain\nthe information;\n\nthe likelihood that the persons, or the kinds of persons, who:\n\n(iii) have obtained, or who could obtain, the information;\nand\n\n(iv) have, or are likely to have, the intention of causing harm\nto any of the individuals to whom the information\nrelates;\n\nhave obtained, or could obtain, information or knowledge\nrequired to circumvent the security technology or\nmethodology;\n\n(i) the nature of the harm;\n\n(j) any other relevant matters.\nNote:\nIf the security technology or methodology mentioned in paragraph (h)\nis encryption, an encryption key is an example of information required\nto circumvent the security technology or methodology.\n\nAmendments  Schedule 1\n\nNo. 12, 2017\nPrivacy Amendment (Notifiable Data Breaches) Act 2017",
      "skos:definition": "likely, to result in serious harm—relevant matters\n\nFor the purposes of this Division, in determining whether a\nreasonable person would conclude that an access to, or a disclosure\nof, information:\n\n(a) would be likely; or\n\n(b) would not be likely;\nto result in serious harm to any of the individuals to whom the\ninformation relates, have regard to the following:\n\n(c) the kind or kinds of information;\n\n(d) the sensitivity of the information;\n\n(e) whether the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures;\n\n(f) if the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures—the likelihood that any of those security measures\ncould be overcome;\n\n(g) the persons, or the kinds of persons, who have obtained, or\nwho could obtain, the information;\n\n(h) if a security technology or methodology:\n\n(i) was used in relation to the information; and\n\n(ii) was designed to make the information unintelligible or\nmeaningless to persons who are not authorised to obtain\nthe information;\n\nthe likelihood that the persons, or the kinds of persons, who:\n\n(iii) have obtained, or who could obtain, the information;\nand\n\n(iv) have, or are likely to have, the intention of causing harm\nto any of the individuals to whom the information\nrelates;\n\nhave obtained, or could obtain, information or knowledge\nrequired to circumvent the security technology or\nmethodology;\n\n(i) the nature of the harm;\n\n(j) any other relevant matters.\nNote:\nIf the security technology or methodology mentioned in paragraph (h)\nis encryption, an encryption key is an example of information required\nto circumvent the security technology or methodology.\n\nAmendments  Schedule 1\n\nNo. 12, 2017\nPrivacy Amendment (Notifiable Data Breaches) Act 2017",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017-sch-1-sec-26wg-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017-sch-1-sec-26wg-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017-sch-1-sec-26wg-logic-alleninterval-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "dfc0e97e53178d3d104afb57693c1ff9b30cba647db540f8e929ac5234ebef96",
      "skos:note": "Determine likelihood of serious harm from access or disclosure of information"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/privacy-amendment-notifiable-data-breaches-act-2017#sch-1-sec-26wg",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "26WG Whether access or disclosure would be likely, or would not be",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 14,
      "values:originalText": "likely, to result in serious harm—relevant matters\n\nFor the purposes of this Division, in determining whether a\nreasonable person would conclude that an access to, or a disclosure\nof, information:\n\n(a) would be likely; or\n\n(b) would not be likely;\nto result in serious harm to any of the individuals to whom the\ninformation relates, have regard to the following:\n\n(c) the kind or kinds of information;\n\n(d) the sensitivity of the information;\n\n(e) whether the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures;\n\n(f) if the information is protected by one or more security\nmeasures—the likelihood that any of those security measures\ncould be overcome;\n\n(g) the persons, or the kinds of persons, who have obtained, or\nwho could obtain, the information;\n\n(h) if a security technology or methodology:\n\n(i) was used in relation to the information; and\n\n(ii) was designed to make the information unintelligible or\nmeaningless to persons who are not authorised to obtain\nthe information;\n\nthe likelihood that the persons, or the kinds of persons, who:\n\n(iii) have obtained, or who could obtain, the information;\nand\n\n(iv) have, or are likely to have, the intention of causing harm\nto any of the individuals to whom the information\nrelates;\n\nhave obtained, or could obtain, information or knowledge\nrequired to circumvent the security technology or\nmethodology;\n\n(i) the nature of the harm;\n\n(j) any other relevant matters.\nNote:\nIf the security technology or methodology mentioned in paragraph (h)\nis encryption, an encryption key is an example of information required\nto circumvent the security technology or methodology.\n\nAmendments  Schedule 1\n\nNo. 12, 2017\nPrivacy Amendment (Notifiable Data Breaches) Act 2017"
    },
    {
